.TH TPA 1 "August 2026" "TPA Manual" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
tpa \- build Debian packages and verified APT repository trees
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tpa
.I command
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B tpa
identifies itself as TPA (Tool for Package Automation). A normal source build
reports version dev. Official builds inject one Debian-valid TPA_VERSION into
the CLI and package provenance. General help includes
https://github.com/eugen252009/tpa and support contact tpa@lupricht.net.
.PP
.B tpa
creates Debian package directory trees, uses
.BR dpkg-deb (1)
to build package archives, and derives APT repository metadata from actual .deb
artifacts. Plain, gzip-compressed, and xz-compressed control archives are normally
read in process; explicitly unsupported formats use a bounded dpkg-deb fallback.
Malformed supported archives fail directly.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B init
Create a package tree with DEBIAN/control and maintainer scripts.
.TP
.B build
Build a .deb package with dpkg-deb.
.TP
.B parse
Read control metadata from a .deb package. Plain, gzip-compressed, and
xz-compressed control archives are read in process. Explicitly unsupported
formats fall back to dpkg-deb; malformed supported archives are rejected.
.TP
.B pack
Generate and verify Packages, Packages.gz, Release, and optionally InRelease.
.TP
.B inspect
Read repository Release metadata and Packages indexes from a local path or read-only HTTP(S)/SSH locator. Supports human or JSON output and exact package identity lookup.
.TP
.B verify
Verify repository Release/index checksums, optional InRelease signature, and each indexed .deb artifact's size, digest, and package identity.
.TP
.B unlist
Remove one exact package identity from its Packages index, regenerate and
verify repository metadata, and atomically publish the result. The .deb remains
in the pool.
.TP
.B delete
Require explicit confirmation, unlist the exact package if necessary, verify
and atomically publish metadata, then remove its physical .deb artifact.
.TP
.B json
Read package initialization configuration from standard input.
.TP
.B schema
Print the TypeScript-style JSON configuration interface.
.TP
.B version
Print only the TPA program version, suitable for scripts.
.TP
.B --version
Print the human-readable program identity and version. The legacy -version spelling is also accepted.
.TP
.B --help
Print general help, command descriptions, project URL, and support contact. The aliases -h and help are also accepted. Invoking tpa with no command prints the same help and returns a usage status.
.SH IMPORTANT OPTIONS
.TP
.BI -in " path"
Input package tree, .deb path, or directory of .deb artifacts, depending on the command.
.TP
.BI -out " path"
Non-atomic output path.
.TP
.BI -atomic-publish " path"
For pack, build and verify a sibling staging tree and atomically publish it at path on Linux.
Use this mode when replacing a repository that may be read concurrently.
.TP
.BI -gpg " selector"
Select one GPG secret key. Full fingerprints are recommended. Empty means unsigned.
For lifecycle commands on a signed repository, supply the key that verifies its
current InRelease. TPA uses the caller's GPG environment, including GNUPGHOME.
.TP
.BI -package " name"
Package name for unlist or delete. Combine with -ver and -arch to select one exact identity.
.TP
.BI -ver " version"
Exact package version for unlist or delete.
.TP
.BI -arch " architecture"
Exact package architecture for unlist or delete; required even when the same
Package and Version exist for other architectures.
.TP
.BI -keyring " path"
For inspect/verify, provide an exported public OpenPGP keyring for InRelease verification. Secret-key material is rejected.
.TP
.BI -fingerprint " fingerprint"
For inspect/verify, require the specified full signer fingerprint.
.TP
.B --require-signed
For inspect/verify, require and cryptographically verify a signed InRelease.
.TP
.B --json
For inspect/verify, emit one JSON document to standard output; errors remain machine-readable and return non-zero.
.TP
.BI -codename " name"
Select the distribution under dists/ for inspect/verify; default is stable.
.TP
.B --yes
For delete only, explicitly approve permanent deletion in non-interactive use.
Without --yes, delete requires a terminal and only y or yes (case-insensitive)
approves; the default answer is no.
.TP
.BI -workers " count"
For pack, bound package inspection, source hashing during pool copy, and
published-artifact verification concurrency. Zero (the default) uses GOMAXPROCS,
capped at 32.
.TP
.BI -generation-manifest " path"
After building and verifying a repository, write a deterministic complete-file
inventory outside the repository tree. Requires -repository-id and -generation-id.
Version 1 manifests are limited to 16 MiB and 65,536 files.
.TP
.BI -repository-id " id"
Repository identity included in a generation manifest.
.TP
.BI -generation-id " id"
Generation identity included in a generation manifest.
.TP
.BI -parent-generation " id"
Optional expected parent generation included in a generation manifest.
.TP
.BI -depends " relationships"
Set Depends in a generated package control file.
.TP
.BI -pre-depends " relationships"
Set Pre-Depends.
.TP
.BI -recommends " relationships"
Set Recommends.
.TP
.BI -suggests " relationships"
Set Suggests.
.TP
.BI -provides " relationships"
Set Provides.
.TP
.BI -conflicts " relationships"
Set Conflicts.
.TP
.BI -breaks " relationships"
Set Breaks.
.TP
.BI -replaces " relationships"
Set Replaces.
.TP
.BI -preinst " body"
Write the DEBIAN/preinst maintainer script body.
.TP
.BI -postinst " body"
Write the DEBIAN/postinst maintainer script body.
.TP
.BI -prerm " body"
Write the DEBIAN/prerm maintainer script body.
.TP
.BI -postrm " body"
Write the DEBIAN/postrm maintainer script body.
.SH JSON CONTROL METADATA
Known package fields remain typed, while additional scalar fields inside
.B control
are accepted without source changes. Keys are converted generically to Debian
field names, for example packageType becomes Package-Type and memaSchema becomes
Mema-Schema. Strings, numbers, and booleans are supported; arrays, objects,
nulls, unsafe names, control characters, and normalized field-name collisions
are rejected.
.PP
Maintainer scripts belong in the separate
.B scripts
object with preinst, postinst, prerm, and postrm values. Legacy *body fields are
accepted for compatibility and normalized into that object; script bodies are
never emitted as control fields. TPA adds TPA-Version and UTC RFC3339
Created-At when they are not supplied explicitly. Explicit equivalent values win;
normalized duplicates are rejected. The --no-provenance option, or provenance: false
in JSON configuration, disables only automatic generation and preserves explicit
metadata.
.SH REPOSITORY CORRECTNESS
The .deb artifacts are authoritative package state. Packages, Release, and InRelease
are derived state; no persistent package database is required. The package control stanza
is read from each actual .deb and preserved in Packages. Package-provided Filename, Size,
and SHA256 fields are replaced by values derived from the published artifact.
TPA verifies package bytes and Release index hashes before success. Signed repositories
additionally require a valid InRelease signature from the selected full fingerprint and
a signed payload identical to Release.
.PP
Package identity is Package plus Version plus Architecture. Byte-identical duplicate
artifacts are indexed once. Conflicting artifacts with the same identity fail generation.
.PP
The input artifact directory is the desired repository snapshot. An artifact included in
a fresh input snapshot is included in the generated repository; an omitted artifact is
absent. Historical versions remain available only while their .deb artifacts remain in
that input set. Use a new or empty direct output path for exact snapshot generation.
.SH READ-ONLY INSPECTION AND VERIFICATION
.B inspect
and
.B verify
take one positional repository locator. Local directories, HTTP(S) URLs, and absolute ssh:// URLs are supported; a no-scheme host-like locator defaults to HTTPS. Existing relative directories and explicit ./ or ../ paths are local. HTTPS redirects cannot downgrade to HTTP. SSH reads use the system ssh client in batch mode with host-key checking enabled and only cat the requested repository file.
.PP
.B inspect
checks Release-declared plain, gzip, or xz Packages index sizes and SHA256 values and reports every Debian control field. It does not fetch .deb archives. A present InRelease signature is reported as unverified unless -keyring is supplied. Exact lookup requires -package, -ver, and -arch together.
.PP
.B verify
checks all listed package artifacts, including indexed size, SHA256, and the package identity parsed from each .deb. A present InRelease must verify against supplied public keys; -require-signed also rejects unsigned repositories. -fingerprint optionally pins the signer. Detached Release.gpg signatures are unsupported. Release files are limited to 32 MiB, indexes to 256 MiB, keyrings to 1 MiB, and reports to 100,000 package identities. Each .deb is limited to 16 GiB; concurrent temporary archive storage is budgeted to 2 GiB, with a larger single archive processed exclusively. Temporary files are removed after verification.
.SH PACKAGE LIFECYCLE
.B unlist
removes exactly one Package+Version+Architecture identity from the selected
component and architecture index. It regenerates Packages.gz and Release,
re-signs InRelease when applicable, verifies the complete candidate repository,
and atomically publishes it. The .deb remains physically present in pool and
may still be directly downloaded. Unlisting does not uninstall packages from
existing client systems.
.PP
.B delete
is the confirmed destructive operation. When the identity is listed, TPA first
performs the same verified unlist transition. When it is already unlisted, TPA
skips metadata mutation. In both cases the .deb is removed only after metadata
has been verified and safely published. If metadata generation, signing,
verification, or publication fails, the artifact remains. If physical cleanup
fails after unlisting, the repository remains valid and unlisted while the
artifact remains. TPA does not track retained historical generations; that
retention boundary belongs to an orchestrator such as TPA.run.
.PP
Use -in for the repository tree, -package, -ver, and -arch for the exact
identity, and -codename/-components when the repository does not use stable/main.
Signed repositories require -gpg with the current signing key; omit it for an
unsigned repository. A fresh pack from an input artifact set that includes an
unlisted .deb will list it again.
.SH ATOMIC PUBLICATION
Atomic publication uses a fresh sibling staging directory, complete generation and
verification, a Linux rename exchange, and replaced-tree cleanup. A persistent hidden
sibling lock file serializes TPA atomic writers and lifecycle changes; do not remove it
while TPA writers may be active. Failure before exchange leaves the previous live
repository unchanged. Repository data is published readable but not writable by readers;
GPG key material is not stored in the repository tree.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
tpa init -name=meta -ver=1.0 -arch=all \
  -maintainer='Example <example@example.invalid>' \
  -desc='Meta package' -depends='base (= 1.0)' -out=build/meta

tpa build -in=build/meta -out=dist/meta_1.0_all.deb

tpa pack -in=dist -atomic-publish=/srv/apt/example \
  -gpg=FULL_SIGNING_FINGERPRINT

tpa unlist -in=/srv/apt/example -package=foo -ver=1.2.3 -arch=amd64 \
  -gpg=FULL_SIGNING_FINGERPRINT

tpa delete -in=/srv/apt/example -package=foo -ver=1.2.3 -arch=amd64 \
  -gpg=FULL_SIGNING_FINGERPRINT --yes
.fi
.SH EXIT STATUS
Zero indicates success or a confirmed no-op cancellation of delete. Invalid input,
unknown commands, build failures, verification failures, and signing failures return
non-zero. Diagnostics are written to standard error.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR dpkg-deb (1),
.BR apt-get (8),
.BR gzip (1),
.BR gpg (1)
